From: Nickolay Semendyaev <agent00791@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] libbt: fm/bt via v4l2, restored for P

On this board the controller is not libbt's to bring up. It sits behind a
shared line discipline in the kernel, and three drivers register with it:
Bluetooth (bt_protocol_driver), FM radio and ANT, none of the last two
having a vendor library of any kind. Whoever registers first makes the
line discipline install itself, and installing it downloads the patchram.
That is why the download lives in the kernel: it has to serve a radio
that libbt never hears about.

libbt knows none of this. It opens /dev/brcm_bt_drv, reads the local name
off a controller the kernel has already patched, finds the same .hcd in
its configuration and downloads it a second time. The second pass wedges
the controller: the HCI_Reset that bluedroid sends next goes unanswered,
the stack gives up after two seconds, restarts, and the whole thing comes
round again every sixteen seconds for as long as the adapter is on.

Upstream carries the cure and always has. Commit 0b071ba, "Broadcom BT:
Add support fm/bt via v4l2", guards exactly these two places behind
BLUEDROID_ENABLE_V4L2 and turns the flag on for boards that set
BOARD_HAVE_BCM_FM -- which this one does, and has since 14.1. The patch
rode cm-14.1 and lineage-15.1, and the P branch was re-cut without it; no
commit removed it, it simply never arrived. Bluetooth working on 15.1 and
dying on 16.0 is that gap, nothing else.

Restored verbatim against the 16.0 sources. With the flag in place
hw_config_start answers the firmware-configured callback at once and
sends no HCI at all, and power control becomes a no-op, which is correct
here: the rfkill node belongs to brcm-uim-sysfs. Measured on the device,
firmware configuration falls from 14 s to 0.04 s and the restart loop
stops.

lineage-17.1 is missing the same commit and will need the same patch.

diff --git a/Android.mk b/Android.mk
index 07285ee..96ea0a5 100644
--- a/Android.mk
+++ b/Android.mk
@@ -18,6 +18,10 @@ ifeq ($(strip $(USE_BLUETOOTH_BCM4343)),true)
 LOCAL_CFLAGS += -DUSE_BLUETOOTH_BCM4343
 endif
 
+ifeq ($(BOARD_HAVE_BCM_FM), true)
+LOCAL_CFLAGS += -DBLUEDROID_ENABLE_V4L2
+endif
+
 LOCAL_CFLAGS += \
         -Wall \
         -Werror \
diff --git a/src/bt_vendor_brcm.c b/src/bt_vendor_brcm.c
index d95c398..0611427 100644
--- a/src/bt_vendor_brcm.c
+++ b/src/bt_vendor_brcm.c
@@ -149,6 +149,7 @@ static int op(bt_vendor_opcode_t opcode, void *param)
     switch(opcode)
     {
         case BT_VND_OP_POWER_CTRL:
+#ifndef BLUEDROID_ENABLE_V4L2
             {
                 int *state = (int *) param;
                 upio_set_bluetooth_power(UPIO_BT_POWER_OFF);
@@ -164,6 +165,7 @@ static int op(bt_vendor_opcode_t opcode, void *param)
                     hw_lpm_set_wake_state(false);
                 }
             }
+#endif
             break;
 
         case BT_VND_OP_FW_CFG:
diff --git a/src/hardware.c b/src/hardware.c
index 2a2ac66..087ac7a 100644
--- a/src/hardware.c
+++ b/src/hardware.c
@@ -1324,6 +1324,9 @@ void hw_config_start(void)
     hw_cfg_cb.fw_fd = -1;
     hw_cfg_cb.f_set_baud_2 = FALSE;
 
+#ifdef BLUEDROID_ENABLE_V4L2
+    bt_vendor_cbacks->fwcfg_cb(BT_VND_OP_RESULT_SUCCESS);
+#else
     /* Start from sending HCI_RESET */
 
     if (bt_vendor_cbacks)
@@ -1355,6 +1358,7 @@ void hw_config_start(void)
             bt_vendor_cbacks->fwcfg_cb(BT_VND_OP_RESULT_FAIL);
         }
     }
+#endif
 }
 
